@extends('layouts.app')

@section('title')Deploying@endsection
@section('description')How to deploy an Ignite app: the migrate step, the release build and its secret key, trusted proxies, backups, and the health check the panel does not have yet.@endsection

@section('content')
  <h1>{{ $title }}</h1>
  <p>A deploy runs the migrations, builds the app in release mode, sets its environment and keeps backups. Ignite does not migrate when the app starts, so the migrate step is part of every deploy.</p>

  <h2>Migrations</h2>
  <p>Run the panel's migrations before the first start, and on every deploy after that. In an app, the command is:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-bash">cargo run --bin ignite -- migrate</code></pre>
  <p>Running it again does nothing when the migrations are already applied. See <a href="/docs/install">Installation</a> for the <code>ignite</code> binary.</p>

  <h2>Release build</h2>
  <p>Build with <code>cargo build --release</code>. A release build renders compiled views, so the deployed app needs no view files.</p>
  <p>A release build needs <code>IGNITE_SECRET_KEY</code>, a base64 string of 32 bytes, and stops at startup without a valid one. Create the key with <code>openssl rand -base64 32</code>, and set it in the server's environment, not in git.</p>
  <p>Cookies are marked Secure only in release builds. A release build therefore needs HTTPS for sign-in to work in a browser. Local testing over plain HTTP needs a debug build.</p>

  <h2>Trusted proxies</h2>
  <p>If the app runs behind a reverse proxy, set <code>IGNITE_TRUSTED_PROXIES</code> to the proxy's address. It takes comma-separated IP addresses or CIDR ranges. Without it, the login throttle counts the proxy's address, so every client behind the proxy shares one count per email. Forwarded headers (<code>X-Forwarded-For</code> for the client address, <code>X-Forwarded-Proto</code> for Secure cookies) are read only from a trusted proxy, so list only the proxies you run. See <a href="/docs/panel#login-throttle">Panel</a> for how the throttle counts.</p>

  <h2>Client address in your own routes</h2>
  <p>The panel reads the client address from the socket and the trusted-proxy rule above. Rocket's <code>request.client_ip()</code>, which an app's own routes may call, reads the <code>X-Real-IP</code> header by default, and a client can send that header itself. Turn that default off in the app's configuration, so <code>client_ip()</code> returns the socket address:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-toml">[default]
ip_header = false</code></pre>

  <h2>Development and production builds</h2>
  <p>A debug build, from <code>cargo run</code>, is for development. It reads the views from disk on each request, so an edit shows on refresh. It shows detailed error pages, and it does not mark cookies Secure, so plain HTTP works. A release build, from <code>cargo build --release</code>, is for production. It compiles the views into the binary, shows a plain error page, and marks cookies Secure.</p>
  <p>Run production in release mode. A debug build on a public server exposes its error details to visitors and reads views from disk for every request. Sign-in also needs HTTPS in a release build, because its cookies are Secure.</p>

  <h2>Deploying with GitHub Actions</h2>
  <p>The recommended way to deploy is to build in CI and serve on the server. GitHub Actions compiles the release binary and the assets on an Ubuntu 24.04 runner, uploads them to the server with a key that can only write release files, and then switches the live release with a second key. The server never compiles anything, so a deploy does not use its memory or processor. A failed health check switches the previous release back in.</p>
  <p>The template, with the workflow, the server scripts and a step-by-step guide, is in <a href="https://github.com/lajennylove/RocketBoost/tree/deploy-template/deploy-template">the deploy-template folder</a> of the RocketBoost repository.</p>

  <p>The three files, copy-ready. The same files are in <a href="https://github.com/lajennylove/RocketBoost/tree/deploy-template/deploy-template">the deploy-template folder</a>.</p>
  <details class="docs-details my-6">
    <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-heading">.github/workflows/deploy.yml</summary>
    <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-yaml"># GitHub Actions template: build a Rocket app in CI, upload the release to the server,
# and switch to it there. The server only serves files; it never compiles.
#
# Copy to .github/workflows/deploy.yml in your repository and fill in the values marked
# TODO. See README.md in this folder for the one-time server set-up and the secrets.
name: Deploy

on:
  push:
    branches: [main]
  workflow_dispatch:

# One deploy at a time per branch. A running deploy is never cancelled half-way.
concurrency:
  group: deploy-$@{{ github.ref }}
  cancel-in-progress: false

env:
  APP_NAME: my-app          # TODO: the app folder on the server, e.g. ignite-docs
  BIN_NAME: my-app          # TODO: the cargo binary name, as in [[bin]] or the package name
  EXTRA_BINS: &quot;&quot;            # TODO: other binaries the release needs, e.g. &quot;ignite&quot; for MIGRATE_BIN
  DEPLOY_HOST: $@{{ vars.DEPLOY_HOST }}   # repository variable: the server&#x27;s address
  DEPLOY_USER: deploy

jobs:
  build:
    runs-on: ubuntu-24.04   # the server runs Ubuntu 24.04; match it so the binary runs there
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@@v4

      - uses: dtolnay/rust-toolchain@@stable
      - uses: Swatinem/rust-cache@@v2

      - name: Node for the front-end assets
        if: hashFiles(&#x27;package.json&#x27;) != &#x27;&#x27;
        uses: actions/setup-node@@v4
        with:
          node-version: 22
          cache: npm

      - name: Build the front-end assets
        if: hashFiles(&#x27;package.json&#x27;) != &#x27;&#x27;
        run: |
          npm ci
          npm run build

      - name: Build the release binary
        run: cargo build --release --locked --bin &quot;$BIN_NAME&quot;

      - name: Stage the release
        run: |
          rm -rf stage &amp;&amp; mkdir stage
          for bin in $BIN_NAME $EXTRA_BINS; do
            cp &quot;target/release/$bin&quot; stage/
          done
          # Everything the app reads at run time goes here, e.g. the built assets.
          if [ -d public ]; then cp -r public stage/; fi
          ls -la stage

      - uses: actions/upload-artifact@@v4
        with:
          name: release
          path: stage
          retention-days: 7

  deploy:
    needs: build
    runs-on: ubuntu-24.04
    environment: production   # TODO: create this environment; add reviewers if you want approval
    steps:
      - uses: actions/download-artifact@@v4
        with:
          name: release
          path: stage

      - name: Restore the executable bit
        # Artifact downloads can drop the executable bit, and systemd then fails with 203/EXEC.
        run: |
          for bin in $BIN_NAME $EXTRA_BINS; do
            chmod +x &quot;stage/$bin&quot;
          done

      - name: Install the SSH keys
        run: |
          install -m 700 -d ~/.ssh
          printf &#x27;%s\n&#x27; &quot;$@{{ secrets.DEPLOY_UPLOAD_KEY }}&quot; &gt; ~/.ssh/upload
          printf &#x27;%s\n&#x27; &quot;$@{{ secrets.DEPLOY_ACTIVATE_KEY }}&quot; &gt; ~/.ssh/activate
          printf &#x27;%s\n&#x27; &quot;$@{{ secrets.DEPLOY_KNOWN_HOSTS }}&quot; &gt; ~/.ssh/known_hosts
          chmod 600 ~/.ssh/upload ~/.ssh/activate ~/.ssh/known_hosts

      - name: Upload the release
        # The upload key can only write into this app&#x27;s releases folder, under the commit id.
        run: |
          rsync -az -e &quot;ssh -i $HOME/.ssh/upload -o UserKnownHostsFile=$HOME/.ssh/known_hosts&quot; \
            stage/ &quot;$DEPLOY_USER@@$DEPLOY_HOST:$GITHUB_SHA/&quot;

      - name: Activate the release
        # The activation key runs activate.sh for this app. The commit id is the command.
        run: |
          ssh -i &quot;$HOME/.ssh/activate&quot; -o UserKnownHostsFile=&quot;$HOME/.ssh/known_hosts&quot; \
            &quot;$DEPLOY_USER@@$DEPLOY_HOST&quot; &quot;$GITHUB_SHA&quot;</code></pre>
  </details>
  <details class="docs-details my-6">
    <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-heading">activate.sh, on the server at /home/deploy/bin/activate.sh</summary>
    <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-bash">#!/bin/bash
# Activates one uploaded release of one app. Runs on the server only, through the
# forced command of the activation key in authorized_keys, which fixes the app name:
#
#   command=&quot;/home/deploy/bin/activate.sh &lt;app&gt;&quot;,restrict  ssh-ed25519 AAAA... ci-activate
#
# The client sends the release id as the command (SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND). The release is
# switched in atomically with a symlink. If the app is not healthy afterwards, the
# previous release is switched back in. See deploy-template/README.md.
set -euo pipefail

APP=&quot;${1:?the app name is fixed by the forced command}&quot;
RELEASE=&quot;${SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND:-}&quot;
if [[ ! &quot;$RELEASE&quot; =~ ^[0-9a-f]{40}$ ]]; then
  echo &quot;activate: the release id must be a full 40-character commit id&quot; &gt;&amp;2
  exit 2
fi

APP_DIR=&quot;$HOME/apps/$APP&quot;
# shellcheck source=/dev/null
. &quot;$APP_DIR/shared/deploy.env&quot;   # SERVICE, HEALTH_URL, KEEP, and optionally MIGRATE_BIN
REL_DIR=&quot;$APP_DIR/releases/$RELEASE&quot;
if [ ! -d &quot;$REL_DIR&quot; ]; then
  echo &quot;activate: release $RELEASE was not uploaded&quot; &gt;&amp;2
  exit 2
fi

PREVIOUS=&quot;$(readlink &quot;$APP_DIR/current&quot; || true)&quot;

# Migrations run before the switch, as a deploy step (Laravel&#x27;s `migrate`).
if [ -n &quot;${MIGRATE_BIN:-}&quot; ]; then
  set -a
  # shellcheck source=/dev/null
  . &quot;$APP_DIR/shared/.env&quot;
  set +a
  &quot;$REL_DIR/$MIGRATE_BIN&quot; migrate
fi

switch() {
  ln -sfn &quot;$1&quot; &quot;$APP_DIR/current.new&quot;
  mv -Tf &quot;$APP_DIR/current.new&quot; &quot;$APP_DIR/current&quot;
  sudo /bin/systemctl restart &quot;$SERVICE&quot;
}

healthy() {
  for _ in $(seq 1 20); do
    if curl -fsS -o /dev/null --max-time 3 &quot;$HEALTH_URL&quot;; then
      return 0
    fi
    sleep 1
  done
  return 1
}

switch &quot;$REL_DIR&quot;
if ! healthy; then
  echo &quot;activate: $RELEASE is not healthy at $HEALTH_URL&quot; &gt;&amp;2
  if [ -n &quot;$PREVIOUS&quot; ] &amp;&amp; [ -d &quot;$PREVIOUS&quot; ]; then
    switch &quot;$PREVIOUS&quot;
    healthy || true
    echo &quot;activate: rolled back to $(basename &quot;$PREVIOUS&quot;)&quot; &gt;&amp;2
  fi
  exit 1
fi

# Keep the newest releases for rollback and delete the rest.
ls -1dt &quot;$APP_DIR&quot;/releases/*/ | tail -n +&quot;$((KEEP + 1))&quot; | xargs -r rm -rf
echo &quot;live $RELEASE&quot;</code></pre>
  </details>
  <details class="docs-details my-6">
    <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-heading">app.service, the systemd unit</summary>
    <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-bash"># systemd unit for one app. Copy to /etc/systemd/system/&lt;service&gt;.service, replacing
# the placeholders in angle brackets, then run: sudo systemctl daemon-reload
# The app runs from the `current` symlink, which activate.sh switches between releases.
[Unit]
Description=&lt;App name&gt; (Rocket)
After=network.target

[Service]
User=deploy
Group=deploy
WorkingDirectory=/home/deploy/apps/&lt;app&gt;/current
EnvironmentFile=/home/deploy/apps/&lt;app&gt;/shared/.env
Environment=ROCKET_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1
Environment=ROCKET_PORT=&lt;port&gt;
ExecStart=/home/deploy/apps/&lt;app&gt;/current/&lt;binary&gt;
Restart=on-failure

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target</code></pre>
  </details>

  <h2>Backups</h2>
  <p>Back up the database with <code>mysqldump --single-transaction</code>. The option takes a consistent snapshot of InnoDB tables without locking them. Include the panel's tables and the app's own tables:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-bash">mysqldump --single-transaction your_database users sessions login_throttles user_avatars your_app_table</code></pre>
  <p>A backup holds password hashes and session rows, so store it as you would store the database itself. A restored database brings back its sessions. A sign-in that was ended after the backup was taken is valid again until that session expires.</p>

  <h2>Health check</h2>
  <p>The app serves <code>GET /up</code> at its root, as Laravel does. It returns status 200 with the body <code>OK</code>, and needs no sign-in. Point the health check at it.</p>
@endsection
